      "lineNbr": 306,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-11T00:55:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "can you put a kerneldoc header on this. It isn\u0027t clear to me why we need this code. Won\u0027t the \"EE_DEAD_TARGET\" cases have command\u003dBR_DEAD_BINDER?. The ERRNO case will have cmd\u003dBR_FAILED_REPLY. So this is really just saying whether there is a valid error here... which is also indicated by an empty string or command \u003d\u003d 0.",
      "revId": "b51c8cddc5a6b55049caab975ce2de695f79cb76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ed51fb1a_b25d6c0c",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/android/binder.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 306,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-11T23:11:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I suppose I thought about the error information in an abstract way first before looking at current error scenarios. So these `ee-\u003ecodes` would be a way to uniquely identify errors and service them accordingly in userspace e.g.\n```\nif (ee-\u003ecode \u003d\u003d EE_RETRY)\n        retry_transaction();\n```\nSo, libbinder could retry the transaction regardless of the command or errno returned by the driver.\n\nThis would also allow _expanding_ into new errors if there is ever a need for it. Otherwise we are tied to current set of commands and errnos, or possibly parsing the string. We don\u0027t want to add `ee-\u003ecode` later on and break the api.\n\nI\u0027m not entirely convinced we\u0027ll need the `ee-\u003ecode` as I\u0027m not very familiar with binder errors in userspace yet. So I\u0027ll defer this to you, I can remove the code if you think is redundant.",
      "parentUuid": "42494ca5_9baf678a",
      "revId": "b51c8cddc5a6b55049caab975ce2de695f79cb76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4dcb32f2_5ef0db28",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/android/binder.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 306,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-12T00:05:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It seems like errno already gives us that abstraction and we can assign some specific actions if necessary. Why not use -EAGAIN instead of EE_RETRY to indicate a retryable issue? Given the driver has to change if any error is re-categorized, I don\u0027t see any benefit to EE_RETRY over -EAGAIN. It seems redundant. I guess you are thinking that combining these means that we can\u0027t express _why_ we had to retry which might be important -- and we wouldn\u0027t want to parse the string. We have a lot of errno space in a u32 -- so if needed we could define new \"extended errno\" values or set a high bit as a modifier.\n\nHowever, we only know of 1 retryable case right now and we don\u0027t have any other cases where we\u0027d use something other than BINDER_EE_PARAM_ERRNO, do we?",
      "parentUuid": "ed51fb1a_b25d6c0c",
      "revId": "b51c8cddc5a6b55049caab975ce2de695f79cb76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f99835f0_09d6f47a",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/android/binder.h",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 306,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-14T15:44:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are multiple ENOMEM cases here and do we always want to retry from userspace for those? Or are we switching the errno in the driver from ENOMEM -\u003e EAGAIN for those that should be retried? I think this is the line of questioning that led me to add the `ee-\u003ecode` because it would be simpler to identify the course of action in userspace. However, I can see how we can handle these and other cases with current command/param values, specially if we can expand param beyond MAX_ERRNO. So I\u0027m onboard with removing `ee-\u003ecode`. Done",
      "parentUuid": "4dcb32f2_5ef0db28",
      "revId": "b51c8cddc5a6b55049caab975ce2de695f79cb76",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
